    <string name="demo_text">A simulated car instead of the adapter, to try the gauges without one.</string>

    <string name="values_title">Values of this car</string>
    <string name="values_adapter_limited">This adapter garbles the longer requests that values such as the oil temperature and pressure take, so they cannot be read with it. The standard values are not affected. A better adapter would be needed for the rest.</string>
    <string name="values_unverified">Values with a number next to them are read by identifiers specific to the Volkswagen group. They have not been checked on this engine: compare them with what you know before trusting them.</string>

    <string name="auto_title">On the display of the car</string>
    <string name="auto_text">Android Auto shows apps of this kind only when Google Play installed them. An APK installed by hand works on the phone, but stays hidden in the car whatever the settings.\n\nThe dependable way: create the app in your own Google Play Console, upload the .aab file of the release to internal testing with yourself as the tester, and install it from the Play Store. Uninstall this copy first, and from then on update only through Play.\n\nAfter installing, connect the phone to the car again.</string>

    <string name="survey_title">Survey of the car</string>
    <string name="survey_text">Asks the control units of the car for every value known from Volkswagen group cars and notes down what they answer. This is how values that are missing above, such as the oil pressure, get found. It takes a few minutes and the gauges stand still meanwhile, so do it parked.\n\nTake one reading with the engine idling, another while holding about 3000 rpm, and if you can a third with the engine warm. Then share the record below.</string>
